<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1754">1754</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali Mardan Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a> was a chief of the Bakhtiari tribe who, after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharids</a>, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1750 and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> installed the young Safavid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-iii">Ismail III</a> as a puppet shah. The partnership soon broke down, Karim Khan defeated him, and Ali Mardan Khan was killed in 1754, leaving Karim Khan the leading power in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ali Mardan Khan was the supreme chieftain, or ilkhani, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chahar-lang">Chahar Lang</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a>. He is first mentioned after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747, as a supporter of Nader's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adel-shah">Ali Qoli Khan</a>, crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a>. In late 1747 Adil Shah refused him leave to take his Bakhtiari men home; Ali Mardan left anyway and inflicted a heavy defeat on the force sent after him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>, he fought Zakariya Khan and the Zand chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> and, after an early defeat, seized Golpayegan, a key point on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. Repulsed at Murchakhur in the spring of 1750, he came to terms with his rivals, and with 20,000 men they stormed Isfahan in May. With the city's governor, Abu'l-Fath Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a>, they proclaimed the 17-year-old prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-iii">Abu Torab</a> shah as Ismail III on 29 June; Ali Mardan took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil">Vakil</a>-e daulat and Karim Khan commanded the army.</p><p>While <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> was campaigning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>, Ali Mardan broke his promises to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, squeezing the city and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa">New Julfa</a>, had Abu'l-Fath deposed and killed, and pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>. Ambushed at the Kutal-e Dokhtar pass by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> under Muzari Ali Khishti, he lost his plunder and 300 men. Karim Khan returned to Isfahan in January 1751, and in a battle in Lorestan Ismail III and Zakariya Khan deserted to him.</p><p>Ali Mardan fought on from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, was beaten again near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a> and withdrew to Ottoman Baghdad. In 1753 he returned with a supposed son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-ii">Tahmasp II</a>, proclaimed as Sultan Husayn II, but the pretender proved unfit and was found to be of no Safavid descent; Ali Mardan later had him blinded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the spring of 1754 Ali Mardan captured two of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s relatives, Mohammad Khan Zand and Shaykh Ali Khan Zand, and tried at his camp near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> to win them over to an alliance. At a coordinated signal they attacked him, killed him with his own dagger and fled on horseback under fire from his men.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was at first allied with the Bakhtiari chief <b>Ali Mardan Khan</b>, with whom he installed a young Safavid prince as the puppet Shah Ismail III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chaharmahal and Bakhtiari Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.33,50.86&amp;year=1744" aria-label="Chaharmahal and Bakhtiari Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4342 4775h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4342 4775h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.33,50.86&amp;year=1744">What was here in 1744? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
